# ICRT dashboard: implementation rules

Vue 3 + TypeScript + Vite SPA. Three products, one static JSON payload, three
subscription tiers. Graded on code cleanliness, layout organisation and
pragmatic engineering trade-offs.

## Scope discipline

This is a small application. Reject architecture that solves problems this
codebase does not have.

Do NOT add, unless explicitly asked:

- Pinia or any global store
- A router (single page, no routes)
- Service workers, offline handling, caching layers
- WebSockets, SSE, polling, retry/backoff
- List virtualization, pagination, infinite scroll
- Factories, DI containers, abstract base classes
- Interfaces with exactly one implementation and no substitution boundary
- Barrel files (`index.ts` re-exports) for a nine-file project
- Telemetry or analytics stubs
- Artificial async wrappers around the static JSON

The payload is imported at build time. There is no runtime fetch, therefore no
loading or transport-error state for the dashboard data. The only genuinely
asynchronous and fallible operation is PDF generation.

## Structure

```
src/
  components/dashboard/           dashboard presentation and interaction
  components/ui/                  reusable presentation primitives
  components/icons/               local icon components
  data/testingData.ts              typed static payload
  entitlements/                    tier types and capability policy
  reports/generateReport.ts        jsPDF boundary
  views/DashboardView.vue          page composition and state ownership
  assets/main.css                  Tailwind theme and shared tokens
```

Dependency rule: `DashboardView` composes presentation and may call entitlement
and report boundaries. Dashboard components may import the static data and the
local score scale. Entitlements and reports never import presentation. Domain
policy depends on types, not Vue.

Keep tests under the nearest `__tests__` folder for the code they cover, not in
one top-level unit-test directory.

## The entitlement boundary

This is the only domain logic in the application. It lives in exactly one file.

```ts
export type Tier = 'basic' | 'premium' | 'enterprise'

export interface Capabilities {
  viewAggregates: boolean
  viewComparison: boolean
  exportReport: boolean
}

const CAPABILITIES: Readonly<Record<Tier, Capabilities>> = Object.freeze({
  basic: { viewAggregates: true, viewComparison: false, exportReport: false },
  premium: { viewAggregates: true, viewComparison: true, exportReport: false },
  enterprise: { viewAggregates: true, viewComparison: true, exportReport: true },
})

export function resolveCapabilities(tier: unknown): Capabilities {
  return isTier(tier) ? CAPABILITIES[tier] : CAPABILITIES.basic
}
```

Rules:

- Capability decisions ask capabilities, never tiers. `can.viewComparison`,
  never `tier === 'premium'`. The profile control may map tiers to visual
  treatment, but it does not decide access.
- `resolveCapabilities` fails closed. Unknown input returns Basic. It never
  throws and it never grants.
- `Record<Tier, Capabilities>` is deliberate: adding a tier is a compile error
  until every capability is declared.

## State

- Derived values use `computed`. Never recompute in the template, never store
  a derived value in a `ref` and mutate it.
- Aggregate stats come from `aggregate_stats` in the payload. NEVER compute
  them from the `products` array. The two describe different sets: the
  aggregates cover 15 tested products, the array holds 3. Recomputing produces
  different numbers.
- `ttr_days` is presentation-only here. Surface it in the chart tooltip,
  accessible table and generated report.
- Product ordering is presentational (descending by score for comparison
  legibility). Do not describe it as a ranking supplied by the source.

## Vendor isolation

- Chart.js is imported in `ScoreComparison.vue` and nowhere else. It owns the
  scale, responsive layout, bar geometry and hit testing. Keep branded drawing
  in its plugins and keep the external tooltip in Vue.
- jsPDF is imported in `reports/generateReport.ts` and nowhere else.
- `generateReport` returns a result, it does not throw:
  `{ status: 'ok' } | { status: 'failed'; reason: string }`.
  Translate the vendor error at this boundary; callers see application
  semantics.

## Access gates

Two gated capabilities.

- Comparison gate (Basic): render `AccessGate` over an empty plot placeholder. Do not
  render the real chart. CSS is not an access control, and product data must not
  reach the DOM for a tier that cannot view it.
- Export gate (Premium): a real `<button>` with `aria-disabled="true"`, NOT the
  native `disabled` attribute. It stays keyboard focusable and announces its
  state. `aria-disabled` is advisory only, so the click and keydown handlers
  must refuse the action themselves and surface the reason. Helper text names
  the requirement.

## Types and mechanisms

Choose the smallest mechanism that expresses the design.

- Function for stateless behaviour. Class only where state, identity or
  lifecycle justify it. There is none here.
- Interface only where a substitution boundary exists.
- Discriminated union where states are mutually exclusive and illegal
  combinations should be unrepresentable (the report result).
- No `any`. No non-null assertions. Model absence explicitly.

## Naming

Names describe intent and domain meaning: `resolveCapabilities`, not
`getCaps`. `ScoreComparison`, not `Chart1`.

## Comments

A comment must explain why, never what. If a comment restates the code, the
name was wrong; fix the name and delete the comment.

Delete on sight:

- Scaffold comments shipped by Vite, Vitest, Playwright or ESLint configs
- Section banners and file headers
- Comments narrating obvious code or restating a name or type
- Commented-out code
- JSDoc on functions whose signature already says it

Keep only for non-obvious invariants, security reasoning, or runtime behaviour
that cannot be expressed in code. TypeScript triple-slash directives are not
comments and stay. The aggregates-are-not-derived-from-products constraint
deserves one.

Before committing: `grep -rn "^\s*//\|/\*" src/ e2e/ *.ts`

## Styling

- Tokens live in `assets/main.css` as CSS custom properties grouped by text,
  stroke, icon, background, brand, motion, size and spacing roles.
- ICRT palette: navy `#003B4C` structural and chart bars, mint `#57E5C7`
  interactive elements and the reference line, pale mint `#EAFBF8` surfaces.
- Tailwind theme extends with these tokens. Arbitrary bracket values such as
  `bg-[#003B4C]` are a defect.
- 8px spacing scale. Type scale 1.2 from a 16px base.
- No one-off colours or spacing values in components. Tokens only.
- Focus indicators must be visible and must not rely on colour alone.

## Testing

Test behaviours, not implementations. Verify what a unit does, never how it
does it internally.

- Unit: `resolveCapabilities`. Each tier maps correctly, unknown input returns
  Basic.
- Component (Vue Test Utils): only components carrying behaviour. AccessGate
  copy, ReportExportButton refusing the action when ungated, TierSwitcher
  keyboard selection, and DashboardView rendering by capability.
  Do not test components that only render a value.
- E2E (Playwright): the three tier journeys, nothing else. The Basic spec must
  assert product identifiers are absent from the DOM. That assertion is the
  executable form of the Part B security claim.

Do not test the same behaviour at two levels. Keep the pyramid the right way
up: more fast tests than slow ones. Do not add a coverage threshold; coverage
is a metric, not a goal. Do not edit tests to make an implementation pass.

Leave zero type, lint or unused-import diagnostics.

## Delivery

- Docker: single multi-stage Dockerfile, node build to nginx serve. No
  Compose, no Redis, no Postgres, no second service.
- CI: lint, typecheck, unit, build and docker build in one job; Playwright
  chromium in a second job. Cheap checks run first.
- Node 22.22.2 in the Dockerfile, CI workflow, README and `engines`. Keep them
  aligned.

## Branching

Trunk-based. Short-lived branches merged the same day. CI runs on push and
pull_request. No merge without a green build.

## Be able to answer, without hand-waving

- What organising principle did I choose and why does it match this app?
- Where does business logic live? Where does I/O happen?
- What are the dependency rules?
- Why is this a function rather than a class? Why is there no store?
- Who owns this state?
- Where are external libraries isolated?
- Where is input validated and where does it fail closed?
- Which invariants are encoded in types?
- What did I deliberately not abstract because YAGNI?
- What would I change first if the category grew to 500 products?
